[{:db/id "q-126915/selection/3faa659336ad58520c77fca4b66141e25c5b30311db932f65ec7808e0c1073f1" :answer/id #uuid "b66d3403-d1ff-5652-97ef-b5fc07e9083a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915/selection/68ca2081c86c800689972f0991220154c1f06d43b73ce484e55da15286c16e32" :answer/id #uuid "cbe30193-140b-5cab-9364-627cb4b6776d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915/selection/64b2810a4f3798e80364d9921d5c49bb9c64b8b500d4f7176f8b38205c4b25fd" :answer/id #uuid "649eb34d-7fa3-5df2-a42d-7cf87bca86b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" :answer/id #uuid "9b02fc59-0ee5-5f88-aa22-a383ea9ef6a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915/selection/9f74a8f49570085872fc34568b60cf7be31a71230f2e238fdd0cba57e2f4f28c" :answer/id #uuid "4fe029fb-17de-5063-8724-d9623056cb9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9a599e-0887-532e-b219-a52806bdc8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126915/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-126915/selection/3faa659336ad58520c77fca4b66141e25c5b30311db932f65ec7808e0c1073f1" "q-126915/selection/68ca2081c86c800689972f0991220154c1f06d43b73ce484e55da15286c16e32" "q-126915/selection/64b2810a4f3798e80364d9921d5c49bb9c64b8b500d4f7176f8b38205c4b25fd" "q-126915/selection/51352eba7e1b25625c7875c43a1a51d3fa8025057837b72a5f9c0695a0e512a0" "q-126915/selection/9f74a8f49570085872fc34568b60cf7be31a71230f2e238fdd0cba57e2f4f28c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126915" :question/id #uuid "6dc83e63-c6ff-5ed8-99ed-5a528dba71ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-126915" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126915/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" :answer/id #uuid "fb22d257-820d-5ede-b92d-0c8eeff56850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection/4b0b4e26bfdefa9229be8845c71a7aecb0582733b6a934ba53565b985646e47e" :answer/id #uuid "365ccbc9-7971-578f-9a76-251ecd88d3ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4x \\\\ 4y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection/f2f11a566909828cb04ae3e3e76d44c02d822a041b54f724aa0bf6106800076d" :answer/id #uuid "c1a5ec29-e85a-52b5-bba2-a4509f38b698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection/3bbe1b390d6e9f3b8ac5329b09a234b0efc2d1005cf212837ee480bff9eb2c45" :answer/id #uuid "89a7e83d-52a7-5da1-861b-3c8170465cb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection/1489f0d7bf2ea023bb45998a5dde8337ddbffa2eebc327e620fe422c92201e5f" :answer/id #uuid "edc8a2ab-9d67-55d5-9581-57269cfea2aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7365734d-b574-543d-a51d-344c06ffd158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126918/selection/1489f0d7bf2ea023bb45998a5dde8337ddbffa2eebc327e620fe422c92201e5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-126918/selection/871c5174e6ae7f2b8b50126c53999470baf0195d9031e74778279cb0f36da414" "q-126918/selection/4b0b4e26bfdefa9229be8845c71a7aecb0582733b6a934ba53565b985646e47e" "q-126918/selection/f2f11a566909828cb04ae3e3e76d44c02d822a041b54f724aa0bf6106800076d" "q-126918/selection/3bbe1b390d6e9f3b8ac5329b09a234b0efc2d1005cf212837ee480bff9eb2c45" "q-126918/selection/1489f0d7bf2ea023bb45998a5dde8337ddbffa2eebc327e620fe422c92201e5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126918" :question/id #uuid "568e518b-6dee-5582-961d-6548648b3418" :question/math-academy-id "q-126918" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=4,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126918/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126918"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection/7cf17f26574e32d96dd8139e4b91c6780b6c68b54d5cd77ec1f89ea8b1a13338" :answer/id #uuid "f0233bce-2486-5715-8aee-ad1a18c30119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection/a3aed51e6235b1516e571016ed0d10434e29e76daeb4d7837e8901f1e3d435a3" :answer/id #uuid "d9110b8e-9b9d-52ee-a249-03487884d952" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection/a1dd338b73054fc8651c4fa6fe5f0963983ccbc284d274bb8e03de4d6fbe114c" :answer/id #uuid "66ee0654-35b2-514c-aa2d-7e0e52f92971" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection/8e06b76a82c32841fd855e0a43606117f16d3103e922b0b49fa0baa0b608d8a4" :answer/id #uuid "dd465ca5-5c1f-5c5d-9ba9-21acd3edeaec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection/931f52f48ce366e506ebdc1c267081a2d30b1dad21810c5819e1b0a54ecc49e3" :answer/id #uuid "85915bab-3d12-54ce-b191-0e08c1c9b676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f9d93d-c77a-5050-bfb0-3e4debb32b90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126925/selection/8e06b76a82c32841fd855e0a43606117f16d3103e922b0b49fa0baa0b608d8a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-126925/selection/7cf17f26574e32d96dd8139e4b91c6780b6c68b54d5cd77ec1f89ea8b1a13338" "q-126925/selection/a3aed51e6235b1516e571016ed0d10434e29e76daeb4d7837e8901f1e3d435a3" "q-126925/selection/a1dd338b73054fc8651c4fa6fe5f0963983ccbc284d274bb8e03de4d6fbe114c" "q-126925/selection/8e06b76a82c32841fd855e0a43606117f16d3103e922b0b49fa0baa0b608d8a4" "q-126925/selection/931f52f48ce366e506ebdc1c267081a2d30b1dad21810c5819e1b0a54ecc49e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126925" :question/id #uuid "625cb004-0662-55ed-95e2-71c62ded171c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126925" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126925/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126925"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126934/selection/9a7e6c0d615e9b744f3e22f1320c84b32692e1f2c745a96cbe114ede9a11cc80" :answer/id #uuid "5e77d59e-2797-5a05-95d2-7d9b1fbc0a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126934/selection/24ed4db1feed7b75c4db70725f81f27269867