r $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126934/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126934"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection/09bf3654e9f69b5ca610dd173b049f1ae56a4f8d9a43c15e18d5c3947d0ec12e" :answer/id #uuid "cc3eff3e-5fa2-5095-8790-394390fa2618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection/69e4591009a94380c5a4fd520c2318f562c77e52d575675825abdfe27211720d" :answer/id #uuid "ff1f5b0d-0276-59b5-a8d2-a0d6ae977da4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection/b555c13be26d82820cb2873723fc2c856335f24883292adb467834f3eceedbc4" :answer/id #uuid "8c87159e-cbb0-5ffb-af3b-915b8d0cbc85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection/c51beed827f06c08863cc7d8e75e3ce77a34b938758ba77f14516402f4129025" :answer/id #uuid "e703cf38-75ce-57ce-81ea-0ddc4a5f5472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection/ed55a7b52d2b7a2f51a42a2b75dc2aa216e43a07251ae04793f3880b315dad4f" :answer/id #uuid "d162f81d-0091-50e9-a98e-01e89ce07f8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5661cca2-9145-58a6-ad51-c10bcd6a1d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-149684/selection/c51beed827f06c08863cc7d8e75e3ce77a34b938758ba77f14516402f4129025" :answer-field/choices ["q-149684/selection/09bf3654e9f69b5ca610dd173b049f1ae56a4f8d9a43c15e18d5c3947d0ec12e" "q-149684/selection/69e4591009a94380c5a4fd520c2318f562c77e52d575675825abdfe27211720d" "q-149684/selection/b555c13be26d82820cb2873723fc2c856335f24883292adb467834f3eceedbc4" "q-149684/selection/c51beed827f06c08863cc7d8e75e3ce77a34b938758ba77f14516402f4129025" "q-149684/selection/ed55a7b52d2b7a2f51a42a2b75dc2aa216e43a07251ae04793f3880b315dad4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-149684" :question/id #uuid "3c1d75c3-aa5d-5947-a4e9-863ab3514ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-149684" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-149684/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-149684"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
